        {
          "path": "_m/g/c/v/y/edinburgh-east-wk/sheep-heid-inn-duddingston.jpg",
          "filename": "Sheep Heid Inn, Duddingston.jpg",
          "caption": "Known in popular parlance as 'the Sheep's Heid', this public house claims to be the oldest known pub in Scotland. Tradition has it that it was established around the mid-14th century. The name comes from the story that James VI, who played skittles in the yard, gifted a snuff box to the landlord which was either embellished with, or in the shape of a ram's head. The present-day pub still has a fully functioning skittle alley which has changed little since it was built in 1882. It is a popular watering-hole for walkers roaming through Holyrood Park.",
          "alt": "Known in popular parlance as 'the Sheep's Heid', this public house claims to be the oldest known pub in Scotland. Tradition has it that it was established around the mid-14th century. The name comes from the story that James VI, who played skittles in the yard, gifted a snuff box to the landlord which was either embellished with, or in the shape of a ram's head. The present-day pub still has a fully functioning skittle alley which has changed little since it was built in 1882. It is a popular watering-hole for walkers roaming through Holyrood Park.",
